  ],
  "text": "Some Roman aqueducts drop a few centimetres per kilometre. Surveyed without a single laser. Water only runs downhill. Get the slope wrong and it stops, or it tears the channel apart. Too flat and it silts up. Too steep and it scours the stone out from under itself. So the channel has to fall by about a hand's width every kilometre, for fifty kilometres. The arches everybody photographs are not the achievement. They are just what you build when the ground is briefly in the wrong place. The achievement is holding that slope across a landscape with a water level and a stick. And when a valley was too deep to bridge, they ran the water down one side and back up the other. Under pressure, in sealed pipe, with no pump anywhere in the entire system. A whole city drinking continuously, and nothing in the chain was ever switched on. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
